{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And the thing was good in my eyes.</b> In my eyes, but not in the eyes of the Omnipresent. And if it was good in the eyes of Moses, why did he say it in the reproofs? A parable: a man who says to his fellow, 'Sell me this donkey of yours.' He says, 'Yes.' 'Are you giving it to me on trial?' He says, 'Yes.' 'In the mountains and on the hills?' He says, 'Yes.' Once the seller saw that he holds nothing back, the buyer said in his heart: This one is certain that I will find no blemish in it. Immediately he says to him, 'Take your money — I am not testing it from now on.' So too I acquiesced to your words — perhaps you would reconsider when you saw that I do not hold back, but you did not reconsider. (Sifrei)" "<b>And I took from you.</b> From the choicest among you, from the officers among you. (ibid.)" "<b>Twelve men, one man per tribe.</b> This teaches that the tribe of Levi was not among them. (ibid.)"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:45:30.332117616Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}